{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2404.13060, why is it insufficient to audit only the final AI product, and what additional processes are recommended?", "answer": "Auditing only the final product is insufficient because risks can be imperceptible in the final model; ongoing process audits, including data and training, are recommended.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "black-box systems also cannot necessarily be evaluated only as a final product, for instance, because training data attacks can be imperceptible in the final model (Khalid et al. 2019) making evaluation of inputs and processes necessary."}, {"page": 7, "quote": "Process audits can and should start with evaluating the company, its structure and governance, and its safety and ethical orientation and commitments, as discussed below, and must extend from the process of data collection and training through post-deployment monitoring."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2404.13060, what are two reasons given for the necessity of ongoing, post-deployment monitoring of AI systems?", "answer": "Ongoing monitoring is needed to anticipate future misuse and to adapt to changing societal impacts as AI capabilities and the digital landscape evolve.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Ideally, ongoing monitoring would be part of a principled and public process that tries to anticipate future misuse rather than reacting as new implicit capabilities are discovered."}, {"page": 7, "quote": "Societal impacts are hard to determine a priori, and will change as these systems develop new capabilities, as society adapts to them, and as the digital landscape changes over time."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2404.13060, what is the purpose of a risk register in the context of auditing a firm's safety culture?", "answer": "A risk register documents which risks are addressed and which have been raised, supporting robust risk management and internal reporting.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "would use risk registers to document what risks they address and which have been raised, would have robust systems in place to address each of the risks, would have systems for raising and addressing internal safety concerns, and would have clear procedures for stopping development or training if concerns are raised."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2404.13060, what is meant by 'safety-washing' in the context of internal AI audits?", "answer": "Safety-washing refers to internal audits lacking broad input, which can give a false impression of safety without genuine accountability.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "internal auditing without broad input about the concerns that must be addressed can easily become safety-washing. (Lazar and Nelson, 2023)"}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2404.13060, what is a potential negative effect of static regulatory limits on AI system properties?", "answer": "Static limits can incentivize firms to approach but not exceed thresholds, rather than genuinely reducing risk.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Specific requirements and maximums imposed by regulation often become a target when they relate to a system property of interest, per Shorrock's Law of Limits. (Shorrock, 2019) In the case of AI, a requirement that systems not score over some limit on a risk scale, or not have a racial or other bias that exceeds some numerical value, would require checking of compliance, and if there is any expense associated with compliance, this produces implicit pressure to get as close as possible to that limit without exceeding it."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2404.13060, what is a risk if AI audit standards are dictated by narrow industry interests?", "answer": "Industry capture could lead to standards that prioritize reputational benefits over substantive safety, risking public harm and backlash.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "industry capture would be disastrous. The challenge of regulation is worsened by the rush towards investments and focus on profits, which will ultimately hurt the industry. Failures, even reasonable ones, would lead to public pushback against even beneficial AI, and such failures are inevitable, especially if standards are dictated by narrow interests."}]}
]}
